# 5. HYBRID DEPLOYMENT ARCHITECTURE

## 5.1 Deployment Model A: Managed Cloud

This will be the default deployment model.

All participating businesses will access the centrally hosted application through a common application address.

Each business must have:

- A unique business account.
- Its own business configuration.
- Isolated data.
- Assigned users.
- Assigned branches.
- Independent business settings.
- Its own transaction history.
- Configurable industry modules.

The central platform will manage the underlying infrastructure.

### Example structure

Central Platform

- Business A — Provision Store
- Business B — Restaurant
- Business C — Cosmetics Shop
- Business D — Bar
- Business E — Pharmacy

Each business must remain isolated from the others.

No business user may access another business's information without an explicitly authorised platform-level process.

## 5.2 Deployment Model B: Dedicated Installation

Dedicated installations will be available for customers who require a separate environment.

Each dedicated installation will have:

- Its own application deployment.
- Its own Supabase project or dedicated PostgreSQL environment.
- Its own authentication environment.
- Its own storage environment.
- Its own configuration.
- Its own backups.
- Its own licence information.

The same source codebase must support both deployment models.

Dedicated deployments must receive application updates through the platform's controlled release process.

## 5.3 Deployment Management

A platform administrator must be able to maintain a deployment registry.

Each deployment record should include:

- Deployment identifier.
- Customer/business name.
- Deployment type.
- Application version.
- Database environment identifier.
- Licence status.
- Installation date.
- Renewal or support dates where applicable.
- Number of licensed branches.
- Number of licensed terminals.
- Maintenance status.

Never store database service-role keys or privileged credentials in publicly accessible frontend code.

## 5.4 Configuration Management

Environment-specific settings must be managed separately.

The application must support:

- Development.
- Testing/staging.
- Production.
- Dedicated customer environments.

Secrets must be stored using appropriate environment secret management.

